foundation.question_text:
The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown. Sketch $y=2f(x)$, marking the images of the two labelled points.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-2.7,0)--(2.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.9)--(0,2.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-2.3:2.3,smooth,samples=61,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.5*\x*\x*\x-1.5*\x});
\node[blue,right] at (1.5,2.6) {$y=f(x)$};
\fill (-1,1) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$(-1,1)$};
\fill (1,-1) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$(1,-1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
$y=2f(x)$ is a vertical dilation of factor $2$: $(x,y)\to(x,2y)$.
$(-1,1)\to(-1,2)$ and $(1,-1)\to(1,-2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-2.7,0)--(2.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.7)--(0,3.7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,gray,dashed,<->,domain=-2.3:2.3,smooth,samples=61,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.5*\x*\x*\x-1.5*\x});
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-2.15:2.15,smooth,samples=61,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x*\x-3*\x});
\node[gray,right] at (1.6,1.4) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[blue,right] at (1.3,3.2) {$y=2f(x)$};
\fill[gray] (-1,1) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[gray] (1,-1) circle (1.5pt);
\fill (-1,2) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$(-1,2)$};
\fill (1,-2) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$(1,-2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

